---@meta
-- vana-client mod API (generated by `vana-client lua-stubs`; the manual is docs/modding).

---@alias vana.Event
---| "load" # The mod's entry file has run.
---| "unload" # The mod is about to be unloaded (logout, /lua unload or reload; a lobby mod as the client closes).
---| "login" # The character is in the world, once per session.
---| "lobby_screen" # The lobby's screen changed (see lobby.state; world once in the world). Lobby mods get it from the start.
---| "logout" # The character is leaving the world.
---| "zone_change" # A new zone has loaded.
---| "settings_changed" # The user changed one of the mod's settings (its Extras page, or /lua set).
---| "focus_change" # The game window gained or lost the keyboard focus.
---| "emote" # Someone used an emote (s2c 0x05A): who, at whom (0 none), the emote id, its mode (motion only, text only...).
---| "party_invite" # We were invited to a party or an alliance (s2c 0x0DC).
---| "examined" # Someone examined us (/check; message 89).
---| "frame" # Every frame (prefer timers and events: this one costs time every frame).
---| "hp_change" # Our HP changed.
---| "mp_change" # Our MP changed.
---| "tp_change" # Our TP changed.
---| "hpp_change" # Our HP percent changed.
---| "mpp_change" # Our MP percent changed.
---| "status_change" # Our status changed (0 idle, 1 engaged, 2 / 3 dead, 33 resting...).
---| "job_change" # Our main or sub job changed.
---| "level_up" # Our main job's level went up.
---| "level_down" # Our main job's level went down.
---| "buff_gain" # We gained a status effect.
---| "buff_lose" # We lost a status effect.
---| "target_change" # Our target changed (nil: none).
---| "time_change" # A Vana'diel minute passed (minutes since midnight).
---| "day_change" # A new Vana'diel day (0 Firesday).
---| "moon_change" # The moon's phase changed.
---| "weather_change" # The weather changed.
---| "party_change" # The party or alliance changed (members joined, left or moved).
---| "item_add" # An item appeared in a bag slot.
---| "item_remove" # An item left a bag slot.
---| "chat_message" # A chat line from a player (or a GM): its text, sender, kind (0 say, 1 shout, 3 tell, 4 party, 5 linkshell, 0x1a yell, 0x1b linkshell 2, 0x21 unity) and whether a GM sent it.
---| "action" # A decoded action packet (s2c 0x028): actor, category, param, info and targets with their results.
---| "battle_message" # A battle message (s2c 0x029 / 0x02D): actor, target, message id and its two parameters.
---| "suggestions" # The client's suggestion rules picked new rows for the modern battle menu (while it is open and a mod listens; none when it closes): a list of battle_rows rows ({kind, id, target, reason, label, group}); held still while the cursor is on one of them, only its submenu's spells retaken.
---| "synth_amount" # The synthesis window's count step (client option synth_amount) was answered: the first synthesis has gone out; recipe = {crystal, items (one material id per unit)} for actions.synth.
---| "synth_result" # Our synthesis was answered (s2c 0x06F): result 0 and 12 made, 1 failed (materials lost); others refused or cut short (the reason's line printed); item, count, grade (0 normal, 1..3 high quality) of what was made.
---| "zone_text" # A zone dialogue line came (s2c 0x036 / 0x02A / 0x027): its id in the install (res.zone_text), its actor, whether MesNum's bit 15 marks it plain, the packet.
---| "damage" # A damage number: a battle message that puts one up (main and proc records), whatever the chat filters show.
---| "ipc_message" # Text the same mod sent from another game window on this machine (ipc.send; needs multibox).
---| "item_change" # A bag slot's count changed.

--- Our character (game.player): its ids, zone, place, vitals, jobs and target.
---@class vana.Player
---@field name string
---@field id integer
---@field index integer
---@field zone integer?
---@field pos {x: number, y: number, z: number} where we stand: x and z on the ground, y down (a smaller y is higher)
---@field heading number radians, 0 to 2π
---@field status integer the server's status: 0 idle, 1 engaged, 2 and 3 K.O., 5 mounted, 33 resting
---@field hp integer?
---@field mp integer?
---@field tp integer?
---@field hpp integer?
---@field mpp integer?
---@field hp_max integer?
---@field mp_max integer?
---@field main_job integer?
---@field main_level integer?
---@field sub_job integer?
---@field sub_level integer?
---@field target integer? our target's server id
---@field focus_target integer? the focus target's server id (/focustarget: one of our party, not us)
---@field last_target integer? the target before the current one, while it is here and can be targeted
---@field away boolean the /away flag (the server's word; /online clears it)
---@field anonymous boolean the /anon flag
---@field seeking boolean seeking a party (/seacom's flag)

--- A character, NPC or monster in the zone (game.entity, game.entities, game.target); ours too, with `me`.
---@class vana.Entity
---@field id integer
---@field index integer
---@field name string
---@field kind 'player'|'npc'|'mob'
---@field pos {x: number, y: number, z: number} x and z on the ground, y down
---@field heading number radians, 0 to 2π
---@field distance number from us, in yalms
---@field hpp integer
---@field gauge boolean it has an HP gauge (the target window shows one)
---@field status integer
---@field speed number
---@field claim integer
---@field targetable boolean
---@field hidden boolean
---@field master integer the master's index (pets), 0 for none
---@field me boolean? true for our own character
---@field race integer?
---@field model integer a monster or NPC model id (0 for a player-style look)
---@field bones integer its model's skeleton bones: a few for an object (a casket: 3), dozens for a creature; 0 for none (a ??? point, a door)
---@field look {face: integer, head: integer, body: integer, hands: integer, legs: integer, feet: integer, main: integer, sub: integer, range: integer}? a player-style look
---@field in_party boolean
---@field in_alliance boolean
---@field hitbox number the model radius the server gives (actor +0x208)
---@field target_index integer? what it targets (its face target; ours: our target)
---@field target_id integer? that target's server id, when known
---@field pet boolean? true for our pet, which also has:
---@field mpp integer? our pet's MP %
---@field tp integer? our pet's TP
---@field pet_target integer? the server id our pet fights (s2c 0x068), when it has one

--- A party or alliance member (game.party).
---@class vana.Member
---@field slot integer
---@field party integer
---@field id integer
---@field index integer
---@field name string
---@field hp integer
---@field mp integer
---@field tp integer
---@field hpp integer
---@field mpp integer
---@field zone integer
---@field in_zone boolean
---@field main_job integer
---@field main_level integer
---@field sub_job integer
---@field sub_level integer
---@field leader boolean
---@field alliance_leader boolean
---@field buffs integer[]

--- An item in a bag (game.items).
---@class vana.Item
---@field bag integer
---@field slot integer
---@field id integer
---@field count integer
---@field extdata string? 24 raw bytes
---@field locked integer
---@field price integer?

--- An action packet (s2c 0x028) decoded: the `action` event's argument.
---@class vana.Action
---@field actor integer
---@field category integer
---@field param integer
---@field info integer
---@field targets {id: integer, results: {miss: integer, kind: integer, animation: integer, info: integer, scale: integer, value: integer, message: integer, bit: integer, proc: table?, react: table?}[]}[]

--- A window's definition (ui.window).
---@class vana.WindowSpec
---@field title string?
---@field width number?
---@field help string?
---@field hud boolean?
---@field frame boolean?
---@field x number?
---@field y number?
---@field elements table[]? see ui.window
---@field modal boolean? keeps the keys and the pointer while open
---@field popup boolean? modal, opened at the pointer, closed by a click outside it
---@field opacity number? the whole window's opacity, 0..1 (per-window opacity)
---@field edge boolean? an edge window, as the Timers and the training regime
---@field heading string? an edge window's line over its elements
---@field resize boolean? an edge window can be made wider
---@field panel string? the built-in panel an edge window stands in for: 'regime', 'timers_recasts', 'timers_buffs', 'timers_debuffs', 'skillchains', 'minimap'

--- A packet as a hook sees it (packets.on_in / on_out).
---@class vana.Packet
---@field id integer
---@field data string
---@field original string
---@field injected boolean
---@field blocked boolean
---@field outgoing boolean

--- A key as input.on_key sees it.
---@class vana.KeyEvent
---@field key string a, 1, f1, enter, numpad+, lctrl...
---@field down boolean
---@field repeated boolean
---@field ctrl boolean
---@field alt boolean
---@field shift boolean
---@field typing boolean a text field has the keys
---@field blocked boolean an earlier mod took it

--- The mouse as input.on_mouse sees it.
---@class vana.MouseEvent
---@field kind 'down'|'up'|'move'|'wheel'
---@field button string? left, right, middle, back, forward or other
---@field x integer window pixels
---@field y integer window pixels
---@field delta number?
---@field blocked boolean

--- The time (game.time): Vana'diel's date and clock, the moon, and Earth's.
---@class vana.Time
---@field earth integer
---@field year integer
---@field month integer
---@field day integer
---@field hour integer
---@field minute integer
---@field weekday integer
---@field moon_phase integer
---@field moon integer

--- The running mod (the `mod` global).
---@class vana.Mod
---@field id string
---@field name string
---@field version string
---@field api integer the API version this client speaks
---@field path string

---@type vana.Mod
---@diagnostic disable-next-line: missing-fields
mod = {}

storage = {
    ---@type table plain data shared by every character on this computer
    account = {},
    ---@type table plain data kept for this character
    character = {},
}

--- Lines in vana-client.log.
log = {}
--- Handlers for the client's events (see the event list).
events = {}
--- The mod's own text commands, and running the game's.
commands = {}
--- Chat lines and typed lines rewritten or dropped before the client takes them.
filters = {}
--- Rows in the client's Timers window.
timers = {}
--- The mod's windows, in the active UI's own look.
ui = {}
--- Text, bars, images and maps drawn over the game, under the game's windows.
hud = {}
--- The game's own windows, panels and HUD pieces: what is open, what is highlighted, hiding, placing and scaling panels, where they are, the config.
ui.native = {}
--- The modern UI's look: its sprites, palette colours and window frames, replaced while the mod runs.
ui.skin = {}
--- How entities and the camera draw, and post-process passes.
render = {}
--- The game's sounds and music, and the mod's own.
audio = {}
--- Lines, shapes, text and images placed in the world.
draw = {}
--- HTTP, TCP, UDP, local pipes and sockets, and links (the net permission).
net = {}
--- Messages between game windows on this computer (multibox).
ipc = {}
--- Hooks on the packets the server and the client send, and injecting them.
packets = {}
--- The chat log and the chat line.
chat = {}
--- The keys and the mouse: watching, taking and pressing them.
input = {}
--- Key binds that run lines, as the game's own binds do.
binds = {}
--- Calls after a delay or at an interval.
timer = {}
--- The game's state: our character, the zone, entities, party, items, jobs and more.
game = {}
--- Doing things in the game: casting, items, equipment, targeting, moving, menus (actions or automation).
actions = {}
--- Game data from the DATs: items, spells, abilities, statuses, zones, messages, names.
res = {}
--- Files in the mod's data folder (and its own files, read only).
fs = {}
--- JSON text to Lua tables and back.
json = {}
--- The other mods: the list, calls into them, events to them.
mods = {}
--- Shift-JIS and auto-translate phrases in strings.
text = {}
--- The client itself: the window, the clipboard, screenshots, recording, programs, the process, switches.
client = {}
--- USB HID devices by their raw reports (the devices permission).
devices = {}
--- The mod's saved data (storage.account, storage.character) and sealed secrets.
--- The screens before login, for lobby mods.
lobby = {}
--- The settings declared in mod.toml.
settings = {}

--- Writes its arguments to the chat log (dim) and vana-client.log.
---@param ... any values, tab-separated
function print(...) end

--- Loads a Lua file from the mod's folder once (a.b is a/b.lua or a/b/init.lua) and returns what it returned.
---@param name string module name
---@return any
function require(name) end

--- Pauses the running handler for `seconds` (other handlers and the game go on).
---@param seconds? number default 0: the next frame (it always wakes in a later frame, after this one's actions)
function sleep(seconds) end

--- Pauses the running handler for `n` frames: 1 wakes it in the next frame, after this frame's actions took effect.
---@param n? integer default 1
function sleep_frames(n) end

--- Pauses the running handler until `test()` returns true (checked once a frame), or `timeout` seconds pass.
---@param test fun(): boolean the condition
---@param timeout? number seconds; none waits for ever
function wait_until(test, timeout) end

--- Seconds since the mod was loaded (monotonic).
---@return number
function clock() end

--- Writes a line to vana-client.log.
---@param text string 
function log.info(text) end

--- Writes a warning to vana-client.log.
---@param text string 
function log.warn(text) end

--- Writes an error to vana-client.log.
---@param text string 
function log.error(text) end

--- Writes a debug line to vana-client.log (shown with debug logging on).
---@param text string 
function log.debug(text) end

--- Calls `handler` on every `event` (see the events list), as a coroutine that may sleep. Higher priority runs first.
---@param event vana.Event|string event name
---@param handler function its arguments are the event's
---@param options? {priority: integer?} 
---@return integer
function events.on(event, handler, options) end

--- Removes a handler added by events.on.
---@param id integer what events.on returned
function events.off(id) end

--- Adds a command: typed as //name or /name, it calls `handler` with the words after it.
---@param name string without the slash
---@param handler fun(...: string) 
function commands.register(name, handler) end

--- Whether /name is one of the game's text commands (a mod's command may not take such a name; a typed-line filter can leave them alone).
---@param name string with or without the slash
---@return boolean
function commands.is_game(name) end

--- Runs a line as if the player had typed it (game commands, our commands, other mods' commands). Any line but a //mod command is a game action: it needs actions (while handling the player's input) or automation. /lua load, unload, reload, hide and show need mods.
---@param line string e.g. '/ma "Cure" <t>'
function commands.run(line) end

--- Runs `filter` on every chat line before it is kept: return nil to keep it, false to drop it, or new text (and optionally a new mode) to replace it. Runs inline: it cannot sleep or read game state.
---@param filter fun(text: string, mode: integer?, battle: boolean): (string|false|nil, integer?) 
---@return integer
function filters.chat(filter) end

--- Runs `filter` on every line typed or run (macros, mods) before the command parser: nil keeps it, false drops it, a string replaces it. Runs inline: it cannot sleep (it can read game state).
---@param filter fun(line: string): string|false|nil 
---@return integer
function filters.typed(filter) end

--- Runs `filter` on every battle message before it prints, with game state: m = {id (the message), category (the 0x028 action's; nil for 0x029 / 0x02D), value, mode, lines (as printed so far, in-line codes kept), actor and target ({id; name, pc, named, colour where known})}. Set actor.colour / target.colour to a log colour code (text.colour) to draw that name in it; return false to drop the message, a string or a list of strings to replace its lines (not condensed then), nil to keep them. Runs inline: it cannot sleep.
---@param filter fun(m: table): (string|string[]|false|nil) 
---@return integer
function filters.battle(filter) end

--- Removes a filter.
---@param id integer what filters.chat / typed / battle returned
function filters.off(id) end

--- Adds or replaces a row in the Timers window (shown for mods' rows even with the Timers Extra off), counting down `seconds`: options section ('recast' | 'buff' (default) | 'debuff'), group (a heading, e.g. a target's name), icon {kind = 'status' | 'spell' | 'ability', id}, total (the gauge's whole length), fills (toward ready, as recasts do).
---@param name string 
---@param seconds number 
---@param options? table 
function timers.add(name, seconds, options) end

--- Removes the mod's row by name.
---@param name string 
function timers.remove(name) end

--- Removes every row of the mod's.
function timers.clear() end

--- Creates or replaces a window: title, width, help, and elements in order. Element types: text {id, text, dim, colour}; buttons {id, label, items, marked} (marked makes them options); slider {id, label, steps, at, shown}; list {id, rows, columns, at, height} (a row is a string or {cells..., icon = {kind = 'item' | 'status' | 'spell' | 'ability', id}, colour}; columns {title, width, align = 'right'}, a heading click sends 'sort'); gauge {id, label, value 0..1, text, kind hp|mp|tp|cast|experience|recast, style = 'row' (a list row: the label, the text at the right, a thin bar under, as the training regime's; dim when done)}; separator; tabs {id, items, at} (pages: an element's page = n shows only on page n of the tabs above it); input {id, label, text, max} (a typed line: Enter starts and ends it, Escape undoes it); choice {id, label, items, at} (Left / Right turn it); colour {id, label, value = {r, g, b, a}, alpha} (Enter edits it a channel at a time); section {id, label, open} (folds what is under it, to the next section or tabs); timers {id, section = 'recasts' | 'buffs' | 'debuffs', builtin} (a section of the client's timers in an edge window with its panel: the mods' timers.add rows, and with builtin (the default) the client's own: our recasts and effects, the ones we put on others); canvas {id, w, h, draw} (a region the mod draws in, in its own units: draw is a list of {type = 'line', x1, y1, x2, y2, width} | {type = 'rect', x, y, w, h, to (a bottom colour: a gradient), line (outline width)} | {type = 'circle', x, y, r, line} | {type = 'tri', x1, y1, x2, y2, x3, y3} | {type = 'text', x, y, text} | {type = 'icon', x, y, size, kind = 'item' | 'status' | 'spell' | 'ability', id}, each with a colour; ui.update(window, canvas, {draw = ...}) redraws it; a click sends 'click' {x, y}, a drag 'drag_start', 'drag' and 'drag_end' {x, y, dx, dy}, held to the release wherever the pointer goes). chip {id, text, note, tone = 'waiting' | 'refused' | 'quiet', detail} (a status line as the pending-action chip's: a dot, the text, the note at the right in the tone's colour, the detail under it; modern UI only); targets {id, rows} (rows {entity (server id), label, tag}: each the label, the entity's name in its colour and its HP, as the focus panel's rows; a click targets it; modern UI only). Every element may have tip (the help shown while the cursor or pointer is on it) and page. Indexes are 1-based. Window options: opacity (0..1, the whole window: per-window opacity; ui.opacity changes it), hud (never takes the keys), frame = false (no frame), x / y (its place in the classic UI), modal (keeps the keys and the pointer while open; centred unless placed), popup (modal, opened at the pointer, closed by a click outside it), edge (an edge window, as the Timers and the training regime: on a screen edge, the title its tab when folded, heading a line over the elements whose click folds it; never takes the keys; resize lets it be made wider), panel ('regime', 'timers_recasts', 'timers_buffs', 'timers_debuffs', 'skillchains' (the client's skillchain view, whatever its elements), 'minimap' (the client's minimap, and Wide Scan under it in the modern UI): it stands in for that built-in panel, with its place, fold, ui.native.hide and /uimodes; the classic UI draws only such edge windows in the Timers' dock); an ordinary window's panel 'focus' stands in for the modern focus panel (on the target panel, gone while an event takes the target window down) and 'pending' for the pending-action chip's panel (over the cast bar); neither is drawn in the classic UI. It draws in the active UI's look (classic Config page or modern panel).
---@param id string 
---@param spec vana.WindowSpec 
function ui.window(id, spec) end

--- Changes one element of a window (only the fields given): e.g. a list's rows, a text's text, a slider's at and shown. false clears a text's colour or a buttons element's marked.
---@param id string window
---@param element string element id
---@param fields table 
function ui.update(id, element, fields) end

--- A modal question in the UI's own look: the text and buttons (default Yes / No); the handler gets the button's 1-based index, or nil when Escape closes it.
---@param text string 
---@param buttons? string[] 
---@param handler fun(index: integer?) 
function ui.prompt(text, buttons, handler) end

--- A popup list at the pointer (a right-click menu): the handler gets the row's 1-based index, or nil when it is closed (Escape, a click outside it).
---@param items string[] 
---@param handler fun(index: integer?) 
function ui.menu(items, handler) end

--- An element's value as the player left it: {text} (text, input), {at} (buttons with options, slider, list, tabs, choice), {value} (gauge; a colour's {r, g, b, a}), {open} (section).
---@param id string window
---@param element string element id
---@return table
function ui.get(id, element) end

--- Shows a window and gives it the keys (arrows, Enter, Escape); the mouse works in both UIs.
---@param id string 
function ui.open(id) end

--- Shows a window without giving it the keys (a HUD: hud = true windows never take them; a click still acts).
---@param id string 
function ui.show(id) end

--- Hides a window.
---@param id string 
function ui.close(id) end

--- Sets one of the mod's windows' opacity (per-window opacity: its frame and everything in it), 0..1; nil gives it back (opaque). The player's own setting for the window wins.
---@param window string the window's id
---@param opacity number? 
function ui.opacity(window, opacity) end

--- Whether a window shows.
---@param id string 
---@return boolean
function ui.is_open(id) end

--- The window's handler: called with the window id and an event {kind, id = element id, ...}: 'press' / 'set' / 'select' {index, 1-based} (buttons, options, slider, list, tabs, choice), 'edit' {text} as a line is typed and 'change' {text} when Enter ends it, 'change' {value = {r, g, b, a}} (colour), 'toggle' {open} (section), 'sort' {column} (a list heading), 'click' {x, y} (a canvas), 'close'.
---@param id string 
---@param handler fun(window: string, event: {kind: string, id: string?, index: integer?, text: string?, value: table?, open: boolean?, column: integer?, x: number?, y: number?}) 
function ui.on(id, handler) end

--- Creates or replaces a HUD text: x, y (window pixels; negative from the right or bottom edge), text (\n for lines), size (1 = the UI's text), colour and bg ({r, g, b, a} 0..255), align (left, center, right), pad, layer, visible, font ('game' the game's font, 'regular' / 'bold' the modern faces, in either UI; else the active UI's), outline ({r, g, b, a}) and outline_width, shadow. It draws under the windows and hides with the UI.
---@param id string 
---@param spec table 
function hud.text(id, spec) end

--- Creates or replaces a filled rectangle: x, y, w, h (window pixels; hud.screen().ui_scale converts UI units), colour, line (a width: only its outline), round (corner radius; the modern UI draws it).
---@param id string 
---@param spec table 
function hud.rect(id, spec) end

--- Creates or replaces a bar: x, y, w, h, value (0..1), kind (hp, mp, tp, cast, experience, recast: its colour) or colour.
---@param id string 
---@param spec table 
function hud.bar(id, spec) end

--- Creates or replaces a speech bubble: a rounded rect x, y, w, h (window pixels), round (corner radius, default 10), colour (fill), edge and edge_w (an outline round it and its tail, default none, 2 wide), tail (a point {x, y} in window pixels the tail aims at, from the side facing it; false for none), tail_w (its base, default 18) and tail_len (its longest, default 22). Put texts and other elements over it on a higher layer.
---@param id string 
---@param spec table 
function hud.bubble(id, spec) end

--- Creates or replaces a portrait: an actor's head and shoulders drawn live from its model, from in front of its face, lit as the scene: x, y, entity (its server id; required), w and h (window pixels, default 96; size sets both), shape ('oval' (the default) or 'square'), bg (the backing behind the actor), frame (a ring round the edge) and frame_w (its width, default 3), colour (tint). Shown only while the actor is drawn in the world; four actors at most.
---@param id string 
---@param spec table 
function hud.portrait(id, spec) end

--- Creates or replaces a HUD map: the zone's map where the client shows one, x, y, w, h, centre {x, z} (world; default: us), span (map pixels across, 16..2048; default 256), alpha (default the player's Map Overlay Opacity), me (our marker on it, as each UI draws it). Draw other markers over it with other elements at game.map_pos.
---@param id string 
---@param spec table 
function hud.map(id, spec) end

--- Creates or replaces an image from a PNG in the mod's folder: x, y, path, w and h (window pixels; default the image's size), colour (a tint).
---@param id string 
---@param spec table 
function hud.image(id, spec) end

--- Creates or replaces an icon: x, y, kind ('item' (the default), 'status', 'spell' (by spell id) or 'ability' (by res.ability's id: weapon skills, job abilities, pet commands), drawn as the magic and ability lists draw them), id, size.
---@param id string 
---@param spec table 
function hud.icon(id, spec) end

--- Changes an element's fields (only those given); false clears a colour; an image takes a new path. Every element also takes window: the HUD window it belongs to ('hud' by default; hud.opacity sets a window's opacity); tip: a string or {title, text}, a tooltip shown while the pointer rests on it (false removes it); and anchor: a modern panel's name (ui.native.panels), making x and y signed offsets from one of the panel's corners (corner: top_left (the default), top_right, bottom_left, bottom_right), so the element follows the panel, and stays where a hidden panel last was (false: the window again). HUD elements draw under the panels: place them beside a panel, or where a hidden one was.
---@param id string 
---@param fields table 
function hud.set(id, fields) end

--- Removes an element.
---@param id string 
function hud.remove(id) end

--- Removes every element of the mod.
function hud.clear() end

--- An element's handler: a left or right press on it is the mod's (not the game's). handler(id, e), e = {kind, id, button, x, y, dx, dy} in window pixels: 'click' at the release; a press that moves 4 pixels is a drag instead: 'drag_start' (where it went down), 'drag' (at most once a frame; dx, dy from the start) and 'drag_end' with over = the mod's topmost other element with a handler under the release, which gets 'drop' {from = the dragged element}; 'mouse_enter' and 'mouse_leave' {x, y} as the pointer comes and goes. It is the player's input: actions may act here (not on mouse_enter / mouse_leave). Removed with its element (hud.remove, hud.clear) or by hud.off.
---@param id string 
---@param handler fun(id: string, e: table) 
function hud.on(id, handler) end

--- Removes an element's handler (hud.on); the element stays.
---@param id string 
function hud.off(id) end

--- Sets the opacity of one of the mod's HUD windows (the elements whose window field names it; 'hud' by default), over everything they draw, 0..1; nil gives it back (opaque). The player's own setting for the window wins.
---@param window string 
---@param opacity number? 
function hud.opacity(window, opacity) end

--- The window in pixels, and ui_scale: pixels per UI unit (the modern UI's point while it is on, else the classic layout's unit; sizes given in UI units look the same at any resolution and UI size).
---@return {width: number, height: number, ui_scale: number}
function hud.screen() end

--- Where an element was drawn last frame, in window pixels.
---@param id string 
---@return {x: number, y: number, w: number, h: number}?
function hud.bounds(id) end

--- The game's windows open now, by name (menu, items, magic, ability, map, config, status, shop, trade, event, choice...).
---@return string[]
function ui.native.windows() end

--- What the cursor is on in the game window with the keys: {window, kind (item, spell, ability (its name-table id), mount, emote, key_item), id, bag and slot for an item in a bag}; nil when none.
---@return {window: string, kind: string, id: integer, bag: integer?, slot: integer?}?
function ui.native.highlighted() end

--- The game list with the keys: window (items, magic, ability, keyitems, shop buy / sell / wanted / bazaar / wares, auction list / history / sell or an auction menu's name), rows (each its item, spell, ability, mount, emote or key_item id, and count, price, bag, slot, equipped, stack counts where the list has them), cursor and top (the first row shown), both from 1. An auction menu has no rows and no top; its cursor is the highlighted row's child id. Drive it with input.press.
---@return {window: string, rows: table[], cursor: integer, top: integer}?
function ui.native.list() end

--- Whether any game window has the keys (a HUD may hide meanwhile).
---@return boolean
function ui.native.menu_open() end

--- Whether the interface is hidden (the UI-hide key).
---@return boolean
function ui.native.interface_hidden() end

--- Shows or hides the whole interface, as the UI-hide key does. Needs ui.native.
---@param show boolean 
function ui.native.interface(show) end

--- Hides a HUD piece of the game in both UIs while the mod is loaded, or a modern panel by name (ui.native.panels: those that never take the keys; in the classic UI only the pieces hide): party (party and alliance lists, their status icons), target, buffs (our status icons), compass (the modern location panel), minimap, cast (the cast bar), plates (name plates), alliance (the alliance lists alone), log (the log windows: back while an event, a prompt or the chat line needs them), timers, regime (the training regime window), network (the connection panel), palette (the macro palette's drawing; its keys still run macros), clock (the clock slot's line, ui.native.slot), experience (the modern party window's experience bar). Needs ui.native.
---@param piece string a piece or a panel
---@param hidden? boolean default true; false shows it again
function ui.native.hide(piece, hidden) end

--- Fills a built-in panel's slot, drawn by the panel in both UIs as its own text while the mod is loaded (the newest mod's value wins); nil empties it if it is this mod's. clock: {earth, moon_name, moon, next_day, next_minutes} (game.clock's table) beside the game's clock; target_distance: a number (yalms) in the target window; target_hpp: a number (percent) at the classic target window's right, in the HP colour. The target slots show on the current target. battle_rows (the modern UI): up to 8 rows above the battle menu's commands, each {kind = 'spell' | 'ability', id, target (a party member's name: the target prompt starts on them), reason (its tip), label (in place of the action's name), group (spell ids: a submenu on the row, Right or Enter opens it)}; a pick goes through Magic or Abilities and the action's list, then the target prompt, as the player would (the suggestions event's rows come in this shape). Needs ui.native.
---@param slot string clock, target_distance, target_hpp or battle_rows
---@param value? any nil empties it
function ui.native.slot(slot, value) end

--- Places, scales or fades a modern panel while the mod is loaded (the newest mod's place wins): x, y (its top left in window pixels, negative from the right or bottom edge; none keeps the panel's own place, and a panel placed at a point is not moved by the player), scale (0.25..4; the pointer works on it scaled) about origin (top_left (the default), top_right, bottom_left, bottom_right, center: the corner that stays put) and alpha (0..1). nil gives it back. The edge windows (timers, minimap, regime) only hide. Modern UI only. Needs ui.native.
---@param panel string a name from ui.native.panels
---@param place? {x: number?, y: number?, scale: number?, origin: string?, alpha: number?} nil gives it back
function ui.native.place(panel, place) end

--- Where a modern panel was drawn, in window pixels: {name, x, y, w, h, shown}; shown is false while it is not drawn (hidden, or closed), and the rect is where it last was; one a mod hides is where it would stand now (its last size). The chat window, which grows with its lines and folds into its bottom edge when idle, also has room {x, y, w, h}: where it stands at its full height. nil before it was ever drawn or hidden; in the classic UI nil but for chat: the log window (log window 2 with it when that stands over or beside it), its room at the most lines it opens to.
---@param panel string 
---@return {name: string, x: integer, y: integer, w: integer, h: integer, shown: boolean, room: {x: integer, y: integer, w: integer, h: integer}?}?
function ui.native.rect(panel) end

--- Every modern panel a mod may name: {name, about, hideable, movable, shown, placed_by (the mod placing it)} with x, y, w, h once it was drawn.
---@return table[]
function ui.native.panels() end

--- Whether any mod hides a HUD piece.
---@param piece string 
---@return boolean
function ui.native.hidden(piece) end

--- Leaves rows out of a game list while the mod is loaded (both UIs, the list search too): the magic list (spell ids), the ability lists (res.ability ids) or the mount list (mount ids); nil or an empty list gives them back. Using them by command or macro is unchanged. Needs ui.native.
---@param list string 'magic', 'abilities' or 'mounts'
---@param ids integer[]? 
function ui.native.trim(list, ids) end

--- Colours an item's name in the game's shop and auction house lists while the mod is loaded ({r, g, b, a} 0..255; nil gives the game's colour back).
---@param item integer 
---@param colour table? 
function ui.native.item_colour(item, colour) end

--- A game config value by id (the Config window's settings).
---@param id integer 
---@return integer
function ui.native.config(id) end

--- Sets a game config value by id and applies it, as the Config window does (saved with the character's settings). A value is 32 bits, negative for a signed setting. Needs ui.native.
---@param id integer 
---@param value integer 
function ui.native.set_config(id, value) end

--- Replaces one of the modern UI's sprites (gauges, icons, job icons, controls ...; ui.skin.list names them) with a PNG from the mod's folder while the mod is loaded, the newest mod's winning. It keeps the sprite's size and nine-slice insets: draw the PNG at the pack's proportions, any resolution (twice the logical size is the pack's own). false gives it back. Needs ui.native.
---@param name string e.g. 'gauges/hp_full'
---@param path string|false a PNG in the mod's folder
function ui.skin.sprite(name, path) end

--- Sets one of the modern UI's palette colours (text, muted, gold, focus, hp, mp, tp ...; ui.skin.list names them) while the mod is loaded. false gives it back. Needs ui.native.
---@param name string 
---@param colour {r, g, b, a}|false 
function ui.skin.colour(name, colour) end

--- Changes a modern window frame (window_normal, window_focused, window_inactive, window_alert, window_tooltip): path, a PNG laid out as the pack's frame (a square of the frame's size, corners its corner inset, transparent inside; edges tile from the middle), and top / bottom, the interior's colours (any not given stay the pack's). false gives it back. Applies in the pack border style (Settings). Needs ui.native.
---@param name string 
---@param frame {path: string?, top: table?, bottom: table?}|false 
function ui.skin.frame(name, frame) end

--- What can be changed: sprites (name -> {w, h}, logical size), colours (names) and frames (name -> {size, corner}).
---@return {sprites: table<string, {w: number, h: number}>, colours: string[], frames: table<string, {size: number, corner: number}>}
function ui.skin.list() end

--- Gives back every sprite, colour and frame this mod changed.
function ui.skin.reset() end

--- A player-style look's parts drawn in place of the entity's own while the mod is loaded: race (1..8), face, and the model ids of head, body, hands, legs, feet, main, sub, range (as game.entity().look gives them); parts left out stay its own. nil clears the mod's look for it; the newest mod's wins. game.entity() still gives the real look.
---@param id integer server id (ourselves too)
---@param look table? 
function render.look(id, look) end

--- How an entity draws while the mod is loaded: {hidden (model and plate), name (its plate's), name_colour {r, g, b, a}, alpha (0..1, at most its own), tint {r, g, b} multipliers, scale}. nil clears the mod's look for it; the newest mod's look wins.
---@param id integer server id (ourselves too)
---@param look table? 
function render.entity(id, look) end

--- Clears every look the mod set (render.entity and render.look).
function render.clear() end

--- A post-process pass over the world, drawn in the order set, under the screen fades and the 2D layer (nil removes it). The source is WGSL with `@fragment fn fs_main(in: VsOut) -> @location(0) vec4<f32>`; it has `scene` (the frame so far) and `samp`, `depth` (texture_depth_2d), `post.size`, `post.time` and `post.p[0..3]` (shader_params), and `in.uv`. A WGSL error is a red line in the chat log (its line numbers count 15 header lines first) and the pass is skipped.
---@param id string 
---@param wgsl string? 
function render.shader(id, wgsl) end

--- A pass's four parameter vectors (post.p): up to four {x, y, z, w}.
---@param id string 
---@param params number[][] 
function render.shader_params(id, params) end

--- The drawn eye's distance as a share of the game's usual one, as a mod set it (0 when none did; render.camera().distance gives 1 then).
---@return number
function render.camera_distance() end

--- Sets the drawn eye's distance as a share of the game's usual one (not in first person); as set_camera's distance.
---@param scale number 0.5 up to the camera's max_distance (10 at most); no effect while locked on a target
function render.set_camera_distance(scale) end

--- The camera: {distance (the drawn eye's distance as a share of the game's usual one, 1 unless a mod sets it), max_distance, speed (the pan speed's scale), battle_distance, first_person, eye, look (as drawn)}.
---@return table
function render.camera() end

--- Changes the camera (only the fields given): distance (the drawn eye's distance as a share of the game's usual one, for this session; the camera keeps the game's distances for its swing, and the eye stops short of walls), max_distance (the most a distance may be, up to 10; 3 by default), speed (the pan speed's scale, 0.1..5), battle_distance (the distance while engaged, back to the player's after; false clears it).
---@param camera {distance: number?, max_distance: number?, speed: number?, battle_distance: number|false|nil} 
function render.set_camera(camera) end

--- Plays a sound file from the mod's folder (16-bit PCM WAV, mono or stereo, any rate; or a game SeWave file) at the effects volume.
---@param path string 
---@param volume? number 0..1, default 1
function audio.play(path, volume) end

--- Plays one of the game's sound effects by id (sound/win/se).
---@param id integer 
---@param volume? number 0..1
function audio.play_se(id, volume) end

--- Holds the music on a game track by id (the zone's choice of field, battle, night tracks stops until given back); nil gives it back, and the context's track plays again.
---@param id? integer 
function audio.music(id) end

--- The zone's music tracks as the server set them: 1 day, 2 night, 3 solo battle, 4 party battle, 5 mounted, 6 K.O., 7 and 8 from events (0 unset); audio.music plays one of them.
---@return integer[]
function audio.music_slots() end

--- Silences the music while held (true, the default) or gives it back (false).
---@param on? boolean 
function audio.mute_music(on) end

--- Plays an interface sound by id (the menus' cursor, select, cancel...).
---@param id integer 
function audio.play_ui(id) end

--- Creates or replaces a line in the world: from, to ({x, y, z}, entity coordinates), colour {r, g, b, a}. It draws over the scene but behind what stands in front of it.
---@param id string 
---@param spec table 
function draw.line(id, spec) end

--- Creates or replaces a horizontal ring: at, radius (yalms), colour, segments (default 48), filled.
---@param id string 
---@param spec table 
function draw.ring(id, spec) end

--- Creates or replaces an axis-aligned wire box: at (its centre), size {x, y, z}, colour.
---@param id string 
---@param spec table 
function draw.box(id, spec) end

--- Creates or replaces a text at a world point: at {x, y, z}, text, size, colour, outline ({r, g, b, a}); centred there, drawn with the HUD and hidden behind what stands in front of it.
---@param id string 
---@param spec table 
function draw.text(id, spec) end

--- Creates or replaces a PNG from the mod's folder on a world quad: at {x, y, z} (its centre), w, h (world units), facing ('ground' (default): flat, the image's top toward yaw; 'upright': standing, its front toward yaw (read from there); 'camera': always turned to the camera), yaw (radians, 0 east as entity headings), colour (a tint); behind what stands in front of it.
---@param id string 
---@param spec table 
function draw.image(id, spec) end

--- Removes a shape.
---@param id string 
function draw.remove(id) end

--- Removes every shape of the mod.
function draw.clear() end

--- Where a world point is on the screen this frame (window pixels, as hud places take), or nil behind the camera. Not in the lobby.
---@param point {x: number, y: number, z: number} 
---@return {x: integer, y: integer, on_screen: boolean}?
function draw.to_screen(point) end

--- Starts an HTTP(S) request on its own thread: {url, method (any HTTP method; GET by default), body (sent with any method), body, headers, timeout (s)}; the callback later gets {status, body, headers} or {error}. A body of 16 MB or more gives {error}. Needs net.
---@param spec table 
---@param callback fun(res: table) 
---@return integer
function net.request(spec, callback) end

--- Opens a TCP connection (for a companion program); handlers {open(id), data(id, bytes), close(id, error?)} run in later mod phases. Needs net.
---@param host string 
---@param port integer 
---@param handlers table 
---@return integer
function net.connect(host, port, handlers) end

--- Opens a local endpoint another program offers (Discord's IPC and the like): a Unix socket by its path inside the runtime folder (e.g. 'discord-ipc-0' or 'app/com.discordapp.Discord/discord-ipc-0'), on Windows the named pipe \\.\pipe\<name>. The system's control sockets (session bus, systemd, agents, docker) are refused. Needs net.
---@param name string 
---@param handlers table 
---@return integer
function net.pipe(name, handlers) end

--- Listens for TCP connections (on 127.0.0.1 unless options.host); port 0 picks one. handlers {accept(listener, id, peer_ip, peer_port), data(id, bytes), close(id, error?)} serve every connection. Returns the listener's id and its port. Needs net.
---@param port integer 
---@param handlers table 
---@param options? {host: string?} 
---@return integer, integer
function net.listen(port, handlers, options) end

--- Binds a UDP socket (127.0.0.1 unless options.host; nil or 0 picks a port); handlers {data(id, bytes, from_ip, from_port)}. Returns its id and port. Needs net.
---@param port? integer 
---@param handlers table 
---@param options? {host: string?} 
---@return integer, integer
function net.udp(port, handlers, options) end

--- Sends bytes on a TCP connection, or a UDP datagram to host and port. Needs net.
---@param id integer 
---@param data string 
---@param host? string UDP
---@param port? integer UDP
function net.send(id, data, host, port) end

--- Closes a socket (a connection, a listener, a UDP socket).
---@param id integer 
function net.close(id) end

--- Opens an http(s) URL in the browser. Needs net.
---@param url string 
function net.open_url(url) end

--- Sends text to the same mod in the other game windows on this machine (their ipc_message event), or only to one character's; at most about 64 KB encoded. Works in the lobby too (sender ""). Needs multibox.
---@param text string 
---@param character? string 
function ipc.send(text, character) end

--- Hooks received packets (one id 0..0x1FF, or every one when nil) before the client reads them. The handler gets {id, data, original, injected, blocked, outgoing} (data and original are the whole sub-packet, header included) and returns nil to leave it, false to block it, or new bytes. Hooks chain across mods: a blocked packet still reaches later ones. Needs packets.read; changing or blocking needs packets.write.
---@param id integer? packet id
---@param handler fun(p: vana.Packet): (string|false|nil) 
---@return integer
function packets.on_in(id, handler) end

--- Hooks packets about to be sent, after the client built them; as on_in.
---@param id integer? packet id
---@param handler fun(p: vana.Packet): (string|false|nil) 
---@return integer
function packets.on_out(id, handler) end

--- Removes a packet hook.
---@param id integer what on_in / on_out returned
function packets.off(id) end

--- Hands the client a packet as if the server had sent it (it passes the hooks, injected = true). Needs packets.write.
---@param id_or_data integer|string an id, or a whole sub-packet
---@param body? string the body after the 4-byte header
function packets.inject_in(id_or_data, body) end

--- Sends a packet to the server (it passes the hooks, injected = true). Needs packets.write.
---@param id_or_data integer|string an id, or a whole sub-packet
---@param body? string the body after the 4-byte header
function packets.inject_out(id_or_data, body) end

--- A whole sub-packet from an id and its body (the header's size made to fit; at most 508 bytes in all).
---@param id integer 
---@param body string 
---@return string
function packets.build(id, body) end

--- The last packet of an id seen each way, or nil; kept while any mod holds packets.read. Needs packets.read.
---@param direction 'in'|'out' 
---@param id integer 
---@return string?
function packets.last(direction, id) end

--- A PNG of the mod's folder as an inline chat icon: a character to put in chat text (chat.add, a filters.chat rewrite), drawn where it stands at the line's height in both UIs' logs and the classic UI's game-font text.
---@param path string 
---@return string
function chat.icon(path) end

--- Adds a line to the chat log. With a mode it takes that mode's colour, filters and indent, like a game line; {colour = mode} takes only the mode's colour. A mode is 0..255.
---@param text string 
---@param how? integer|{mode: integer?, colour: integer?} the game's chat mode (0..255), or a table
function chat.add(text, how) end

--- The chat line: whether it is open, and its text.
---@return {open: boolean, text: string}
